    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find out how many times Terrell must lift the two 15-pound weights to lift the same total weight, we first need to calculate the total weight that Terrell can lift with the two 20-pound weights.\n\nWeight of two 20-pound weights = 20 lbs x 2 = 40 lbs\n\nNow that we know Terrell can lift a total of 40 lbs with two 20-pound weights, we can use this information to determine how many times he must lift the two 15-pound weights.\n\nTotal weight lifted with two 15-pound weights = (Weight of two 15-pound weights) x Total lifts\n\n40 lbs = (15 lbs) x Total lifts\n\nTo find the number of lifts, we divide both sides of the equation by the weight of one 15-pound weight:\n\nTotal lifts = Total weight / weight per lift\nTotal lifts = 40 lbs / 15 lbs\nTotal lifts = 8/3\n\nSince Terrell can't lift a fraction of a lift, he must round up to the nearest whole number. Thus, Terrell must lift the two 15-pound weights 2 times in order to lift the same total weight as with the two 20-pound weights.",
